# Native port plan, Draft 28, Milestone 7 Task 4 - a genuine, hard-evidence-
# confirmed integration finding, NOT a speculative precaution.
#
# The problem (found via a real crash, isolated to a minimal repro): this
# harness links Core/GameEngine/Source/Common/System/GameMemory.cpp, which
# defines the global operator new/new[]/delete/delete[] (routing every
# allocation through TheDynamicMemoryAllocator). On a normal dynamically-
# linked Linux executable (this repo's default PIE build), those global,
# default-visibility symbols get exported into the executable's OWN dynamic
# symbol table - and because of how ELF symbol interposition works, that
# makes EVERY other shared object loaded into the SAME process resolve ITS
# OWN calls to "operator new"/"operator delete" against OUR definition too,
# not just this harness's own code. This is process-wide, not scoped to
# WW3D2/GameEngine code.
#
# This harness creates a real GLX/OpenGL context (DX8Wrapper::Create_Device
# -> glfwCreateWindow), which on this port's verification environment (WSL2,
# no GPU passthrough for GLX - the same is true of a headless CI runner
# under xvfb-run) falls back to Mesa's llvmpipe software rasterizer, which
# JIT-compiles shaders via libLLVM.so on its own worker threads. libLLVM.so
# is dynamically loaded into the SAME process and also calls plain
# "operator new"/"delete" internally for its own IR/codegen data structures.
# Once those calls get silently redirected into TheDynamicMemoryAllocator
# (whose pools guarantee only MEM_BOUND_ALIGNMENT=4-byte alignment -
# GameMemory.cpp's own "@todo srj -- make this work for 8" comment already
# flags this as coarser than a real malloc's - vs. glibc malloc's 16-byte
# guarantee that LLVM's SIMD-using internals implicitly rely on), the
# result is a real, 100%-reproducible SIGSEGV deep inside
# llvm::MCJIT::generateCodeForModule, confirmed via gdb backtraces and an
# isolated minimal repro (a bare initMemoryManager() + glfwCreateWindow(),
# with NO WW3D2/file-system code at all, reproduces the identical crash;
# removing GameMemory.cpp from that same repro removes the crash).
#
# The fix: this version script (GNU ld's --version-script) marks exactly
# GameMemory.cpp's global-allocator symbols as "local" in the FINAL
# executable's dynamic symbol table - i.e. no longer exported for other,
# separately-loaded shared objects (Mesa's driver .so's, libLLVM.so) to
# interpose onto. This does NOT change how this harness's OWN code resolves
# these symbols: intra-executable symbol resolution (every .o file directly
# linked into this same binary - WW3D2, the GameEngine file-system TUs, this
# harness's own main.cpp) is settled by the static linker at link time,
# before dynamic-export visibility is even consulted, so
# WW3D2/GameEngine/main.cpp's own "new"/"delete" calls are completely
# unaffected and still route through TheDynamicMemoryAllocator exactly as
# intended (verified: initMemoryManager/shutdownMemoryManager and every
# AsciiString/std::string/std::vector allocation throughout this harness's
# checks still succeed). Confirmed via a real rebuild+run of the full
# harness after this fix, not just the isolated repro. Applied ONLY to this
# one target (target_link_options in this directory's CMakeLists.txt) - no
# other harness links GameMemory.cpp, so none of them need or get this
# script.
#
# IMPORTANT: this script must be combined with -static-libstdc++/
# -static-libgcc (see this target's CMakeLists.txt target_link_options
# comment for the second half of this story) - the version script alone
# also breaks std::filesystem (a real "free(): invalid pointer" SIGABRT,
# also confirmed via an isolated minimal repro), because std::filesystem's
# own implementation straddles the inline/libstdc++.so boundary the same
# way, and once our allocator symbols are hidden from dynamic export,
# libstdc++.so's own internal calls to them can no longer find our
# override either - splitting one logical alloc/free pair across two
# different allocators. Statically linking libstdc++/libgcc removes that
# split entirely for this one executable.
{
  global: *;
  local:
    _Znwm;
    _Znam;
    _ZdlPv;
    _ZdaPv;
    _ZnwmPKci;
    _ZdlPvPKci;
    _ZnamPKci;
    _ZdaPvPKci;
    createW3DMemPool;
    allocateFromW3DMemPool*;
    freeFromW3DMemPool;
};
